\label{lem:r=d}
    For $1\le r\le k$, we have 
    \begin{equation*}
    S^{312}_{{r},{k},{r}}=S^{312}_{{r},{k-1},{r}}+S^{312}_{{r-1},{k-1},{r-1}}+\sum_{i=2}^{r} \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}\cdot S^{312}_{{r+1-i},{k+1-i},{r+1-i}}.
    \end{equation*}

Consider a row-complete and extendably $312$-avoiding ASR of size $r \times k$. We distinguish cases according to the first column. If the first column is empty, we may always place a 1 in the extension in the first column below everything else without introducing an occurrence of the pattern~$312$. This yields the first term in the recursion.

    Now assume there is a $1$ in the first column, say in row~$i \ge 1$. There are two possibilities, either the remaining entries of row~$i$ are $0$s or there is a $-1$ in that row; note that the latter case can only occur if $i \neq 1$. See \cref{fig:312_d=r} for an illustration.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[c]{.45\textwidth}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

    \draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (7,5);

    \draw[fill=white] (1,3) rectangle (3,5);
    \draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);
    \draw[fill=white] (3,0) rectangle (7,2);

    \node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
    \node at (-.5,2.5) {$i$};
    \node at (2,4) {$M$};
    \node at (5,1) {$R$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{}
\label{subfig:312_d=r_a}
\end{subfigure}
\begin{subfigure}[c]{.45\textwidth}
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

    \draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (7,5);

    \draw[fill=white] (1,3) rectangle (3,5);
    \draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);
    \draw[fill=white] (2,0) rectangle (7,3);
    \draw[fill=white] (2,2) rectangle (3,3);

    \node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
    \node at (-.5,2.5) {$i$};
    \node at (2.5,2.5) {$-1$};
    \node at (2,4) {$M$};
    \node at (4.5,1.5) {$R$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{}
\label{subfig:312_d=r_b}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{The structure of a row-complete and extendably $312$-avoiding ASR.}
\label{fig:312_d=r}
\end{figure}

First note that if there is a column with only $0$s in the top $i-1$ rows, then there must be a $1$ further down in that column, either in the ASR itself or in its extension. Thus, all other columns to its right must also be empty in the first $i-1$ rows.
That means that, beginning with the second column, all columns that are not empty in the first $i-1$ rows occur consecutively. If in one of these columns the bottommost nonzero entry in the top $i-1$ rows is a $-1$, then there will be a $1$ further down that column, again either in the ASR or in its extension. Since all rows sum to $1$, there is also a $1$ to the right of that $-1$. That would create a $312$-pattern with the $1$ in the $i$th row and the first column. Consequently, columns $2$ to $i$ will have a full $312$-avoiding ASM~$M$ in the first $i-1$ rows. We know there are $\mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}$ such possibilities.

In the first case (\cref{subfig:312_d=r_a}), the bottom right rectangle~$R$ must form a row-complete and $312$-avoiding ASR of size $(r-i)\times (k-i)$, that is, $R\in\operatorname{ASR}_{r-i,k-i,r-i}^{312}$. We therefore get the sum $\sum_{i=1}^k \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}\cdot S^{312}_{{r-i},{k-i},{r-i}}$. In particular, if $i=1$, we obtain the term $S^{312}_{{r-1},{k-1},{r-1}}$ since $\mathrm{Sch}_{-1}=1$.

In the second case (\cref{subfig:312_d=r_b}), the rectangle~$R$ in the bottom right is one row and one column larger. It becomes an extendably $312$-avoiding ASR~$\widehat{R}$ if we replace the $-1$ in its top left corner by $0$.
Consequently, the possible $\widehat{R}$ are those in $\operatorname{ASR}_{r+1-i,k+1-i,r+1-i}^{312}$ restricted to matrices without a $1$ in the top left corner, which are counted by $S^{312}_{{r-i},{k-i},{r-i}}$. Thus, the second case contributes
\[
\sum_{i=2}^r \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}\cdot \left(S^{312}_{{r+1-i},{k+1-i},{r+1-i}}-S^{312}_{{r-i},{k-i},{r-i}}\right).
\]
Finally, note how all negative terms cancel with terms from the first case, and the proposition follows.

\label{lem:k=d}
    For $1\le k<r$, we have
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:312_k=d}
    S^{312}_{{r},{k},{k}}=\sum_{j=0}^k S^{312}_{{j},{k},{j}}\binom{r-j-1}{k-j}.
    \end{equation}

Let $j+1$ be the first empty row of an extendably $312$-avoiding ASR, which is illustrated in \cref{fig:312_d=k}.

    \begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

    \draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (5,7);
    \draw[fill=white]  (0,0) rectangle (5,3);
    \draw[fill=white]  (0,4) rectangle (5,7);

    \node at (-1,3.5) {$j+1$};
    \node at (2.5,5.5) {$R$};
    \node at (1,.5) {$1$};
    \node at (2.5,1.5) {$\iddots$};
    \node at (4,2.5) {$1$};

    \end{tikzpicture}
    \caption{The structure of a balanced                  
    and extendably $312$-avoiding ASR.}
    \label{fig:312_d=k}
    \end{figure}

    In the first $j$ rows, there is a row-complete extendably $312$-avoiding ASR~$R$, hence $R \in \operatorname{ASR}_{j,k,j}^{312}$, which yields $S^{312}_{{j},{k},{j}}$ possibilities. When extending the ASR to an ASM, there will be a $1$ somewhere to the right in row~$j+1$. Thus, below that row in the ASR the $1$s must not form any $12$-pattern. For every choice of $k-j$ rows among the $r-1-j$ rows, there is exactly one way to fill them with $1$s to obtain an extendably $312$-avoiding ASR.

\label{conj:312-formula}
For integers $d \leq r,k$, we have
\begin{multline}\label{eq:conj312}
    S^{312}_{{r},{k},{d}} = \sum_{i=1}^{d} 2^{i-1} \binom{k}{i} \left( \binom{r-2}{d-i} - 2\binom{r-2}{d-i-2} \right) + \binom{r}{d}\\
    + \sum_{i \geq 0 }^{} C_{2i} \binom{r-1+2i}{d-1-2i} + 2 \sum_{i=1}^{d} (-1)^i \binom{r}{d-i},
\end{multline}
where $C_n = \frac{1}{n+1} \binom{2n}{n}$ denotes the $n$th Catalan number.

Let $i$ be the first empty row of an extendably $132$-avoiding ASR. Since there must be a $1$ in this row of any extended ASM, the ASR looks as in \cref{fig:132_d=k}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

\draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (5,7);
\draw[fill=white]  (0,0) rectangle (2,3);
\draw[fill=white]  (2,4) rectangle (5,7);

\node at (-1,3.5) {$i$};
\node at (3.5,5.5) {$M$};
\node at (1,1.5) {$R$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The structure of an extendably $132$-avoiding ASR with empty rows.}
\label{fig:132_d=k}
\end{figure}

    We see that there is $M \in \operatorname{ASM}_{i-1}^{132}$ in columns $k+2-i$ to $k$ above row~$i$. This gives $\mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}$ possibilities.

    Below row~$i$, columns $k+2-i$ to $k$ have to be empty. In the first $k+1-i$ columns, there can be any extendably $132$-avoiding ASR~$R$ with $d+1-i$ nonempty rows, for which there are $S^{132}_{{r-i},{k+1-i},{d+1-i}}$ possibilities.

Similarly to the case 312, we consider the first column. If it is empty, the remaining columns constitute an ASR in $\operatorname{ASR}_{r,k-1,r}^{213}$.

    Now assume that there is a $1$ in row~$i$ of the first column. We distinguish two cases according to whether this $1$ is followed by a $-1$ in the same row, as illustrated in \cref{fig:213_d=r}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[c]{.45\textwidth}
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
    \draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (7,5);

    \draw[fill=white] (1,0) rectangle (5,2);
    \draw[fill=white] (5,3) rectangle (7,5);
    \draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);

    \node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
    \node at (-.5,2.5) {$i$};
    \node at (6,4) {$M$};
    \node at (3,1) {$R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{}
\label{subfig:213_d=r_a}
\end{subfigure}
\begin{subfigure}[c]{.45\textwidth}
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
    \draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (7,5);

    \draw[fill=white] (1,0) rectangle (5,2);
    \draw[fill=white] (4,2) rectangle (7,5);
    \draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);
    \draw[fill=white] (4,2) rectangle (5,3);

    \node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
    \node at (-.5,2.5) {$i$};
    \node at (4.5,2.5) {$-1$};
    \node at (5.5,3.5) {$M$};
    \node at (3,1) {$R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{}
\label{subfig:213_d=r_b}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{The structure of a row-complete and extendably $213$-avoiding ASR.}
\label{fig:213_d=r}
\end{figure}


If there is a column $j$ with only zeros in the top $i$ rows, then there must be a $1$ further down that column in any extension. Thus, all other columns before must also be empty in the first $i$ rows. As a consequence, if row $i$ comprises only one nonzero entry (\cref{subfig:213_d=r_a}), then there is a $213$-avoiding $M\in\operatorname{ASM}_{i-1}^{213}$ in the last $i-1$ columns, enumerated by $\mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}$. If row $i$ contains also a $-1$ (\cref{subfig:213_d=r_b}), then we take the $i \times i$ matrix~$M$ in the top right corner, replace the bottom left $-1$ by $0$ and obtain $\widehat{M}\in\operatorname{ASM}_{i}^{213}$; note that $213$-avoiding ASMs of size $i \times i$ with a $0$ in the bottom left corner are enumerated by $\mathrm{Sch}_{i-1} - \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}$.

In both cases, there is a $R \in \operatorname{ASR}_{r-i,k-i,r-i}^{213}$ in columns $2$ to $k-i+1$ and the bottom $r-i$ rows.

Thus, we obtain
\[
    S^{213}_{{r},{k},{r}}=S^{213}_{{r},{k-1},{r}}+\sum_{i=1}^r \left( \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}\cdot S^{213}_{{r-i},{k-i},{r-i}} + \left(\mathrm{Sch}_{i-1} - \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2}\right)\cdot S^{213}_{{r-i},{k-i},{r-i}} \right),
\]
which concludes the proof.

For $2 \leq k < r$, we have
    \begin{multline}\label{eq:balanced213}
    S^{213}_{{r},{k},{k}}=S^{213}_{{r-1},{k-1},{k-1}} + \sum_{i=1}^{r-k+1} S^{213}_{{r-i},{k-1},{k-1}}\\
    + \sum_{i=r-k+2}^{r-1} \mathrm{Sch}_{r-i-1} \left( S^{213}_{{i},{k-r+i},{k-r+i}} - \binom{i-1}{k-r+i} \right),
    \end{multline}
    with the additional base case $S^{213}_{{r},{1},{1}}=r$.

Let us assume $1 \leq k < r$ and consider a balanced extendably $213$-avoiding ASR of size $r \times k$ with $k$ nonempty rows. Since there are no empty columns, there is a unique $1$ in the first column, say in row~$i$. Now assume that all rows above are empty, see \cref{subfig:213_k=d_a}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[c]{.3\textwidth}
\centering   
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

\draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (5,7);
\draw[fill=white] (0,4) rectangle (1,5);
\draw[fill=white] (1,0) rectangle (5,4);

\node at (-1,4.5) {$i$};
\node at (.5,4.5) {$1$};
\node at (3,2) {$R$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{}
\label{subfig:213_k=d_a}
\end{subfigure}
\begin{subfigure}[c]{.3\textwidth}
\centering    
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

\draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (5,7);
\draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);
\draw[fill=white] (1,0) rectangle (3,2);
\draw[fill=white] (3,3) rectangle (5,7);

\node at (-1,2.5) {$i$};
\node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
\node at (4,5) {$R$};
\node at (2,1) {$M$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{}
\label{subfig:213_k=d_b}
\end{subfigure}
\begin{subfigure}[c]{.3\textwidth}
\centering    
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

\draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (5,7);
\draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);
\draw[fill=white] (1,0) rectangle (3,2);
\draw[fill=white] (2,2) rectangle (5,7);
\draw (2,2) rectangle (3,3);

\node at (-1,2.5) {$i$};
\node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
\node at (2.5,2.5) {$-1$};
\node at (3.5,4.5) {$R$};
\node at (2,1) {$M$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{}
\label{subfig:213_k=d_c}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{The structure of a balanced extendably $213$-avoiding ASR.}
\label{fig:213_k=d}
\end{figure}

    Thus, the rows below form an extendably $213$-avoiding ASR $R$ of size $(r-i) \times (k-1)$ with $k-1$ nonempty rows. Note that the $1$ in the first column can be at most in row~$r-k+1$ to make sure $R$ has at least $k-1$ rows. This gives 
    \begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=1}^{r-k+1} S^{213}_{{r-i},{k-1},{k-1}}
    \end{equation*}
    possibilities.

    If the rows above row~$i$ are not empty, then the extendably $213$-avoiding ASR has one of the forms in \cref{subfig:213_k=d_b} or \cref{subfig:213_k=d_c}.

If there is no $-1$ in row~$i$ (\cref{subfig:213_k=d_b}), then there is an $M\in \operatorname{ASM}_{r-i}^{213}$ in rows $i+1$ to $r$ and columns $2$ to $r-i+1$. The reason is that an empty row below row~$i$ would create a $213$-pattern in the extended ASM. Consequently, $i$ ranges between $r-k+2$ and $r$. Above row~$i$, we have $R \in \operatorname{ASR}_{i-1,k-r+i-1,k-r+i-1}^{213}$ in the top right corner. Thus, there are
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=r-k+2}^{r} \mathrm{Sch}_{r-i-1} S^{213}_{{i-1},{k-r+i-1},{k-r+i-1}}
\end{equation*}
possibilities in total. Recall that we defined $\mathrm{Sch}_{-1}=1$ for that purpose.

If there is a $-1$ in row~$i$ (\cref{subfig:213_k=d_c}), we again have a $213$-avoiding ASM~$M$ in rows $i+1$ to $r$ and columns $2$ to $r-i+1$. In the top right corner, we take the $i \times (k-r+i)$ matrix~$R$, replace the $-1$ at posititon~$(i,r-i+1)$ by $0$, and obtain $\widehat{R}\in\operatorname{ASR}_{i,k-r+i,k-r+i}^{213}$. Since nonempty rows sum to $1$, we know there is somewhere a $1$ in row~$i$ in the last $k-r+i-1$ columns. The ASRs that have a $1$ in the bottom left corner are counted by $S^{213}_{{i-1},{k-r+i-1},{k-r+i-1}}$ and those which have an empty bottom row by $\binom{i-1}{k-r+i}$ since they cannot have any $21$-pattern in the $i-1$ rows above. In total, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=r-k+2}^{r-1} \mathrm{Sch}_{r-i-1} \left( S^{213}_{{i},{k-r+i},{k-r+i}} - S^{213}_{{i-1},{k-r+i-1},{k-r+i-1}} - \binom{i-1}{k-r+i} \right)
\end{equation*}
possibilities in that case. Note that here $i$ can be at most $r-1$ in order to ensure the right number of nonempty rows.

We finally add the three previous sums together. If $k \ge 2$, this sum simplifies to  \eqref{eq:balanced213}; if $k = 1$, we obtain $S^{213}_{{r},{1},{1}}=r$.

Consider an extendably $213$-avoiding ASR of size $r \times k$ with $d$ nonempty rows. We have $S^{213}_{{r},{k-1},{d}}$ possibilities for the first column to be empty because we can always extend a $213$-avoiding ASM of size $n \times n$ by a $1$ in the bottom left corner to obtain a $213$-avoiding ASM of size $(n+1) \times (n+1)$.

Now assume that the first column is not empty and that there is a $1$ in row~$i$. If the rows above row~$i$ are empty, we have $S^{213}_{{r-i},{k-1},{d-1}}$ possibilities to fill the rows below. To that end, the $1$ in the first column cannot be below row $r-d+1$.

If the rows above row~$i$ are not empty, we again distinguish between two cases, depending on whether row~$i$ contains a $-1$, see \cref{fig:213_gen}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
    \begin{subfigure}[c]{.45\textwidth}
    \centering
    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
    \draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (6,7);
    \draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);
    \draw[fill=white] (1,0) rectangle (4,2);
    \draw[fill=white] (4,3) rectangle (6,7);

    \node at (-1,2.5) {$i$};
    \node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
    \node at (5,5) {$R_2$};
    \node at (2.5,1) {$R_1$};
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \caption{}
    \label{subfig:213_gen_a}
    \end{subfigure}
    \begin{subfigure}[c]{.45\textwidth}
    \centering
    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
    \draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (6,7);
    \draw[fill=white] (0,2) rectangle (1,3);
    \draw[fill=white] (1,0) rectangle (4,2);
    \draw[fill=white] (3,2) rectangle (6,7);
    \draw (3,2) rectangle (4,3);

    \node at (-1,2.5) {$i$};
    \node at (.5,2.5) {$1$};
    \node at (3.5,2.5) {$-1$};
    \node at (4.5,4.5) {$R_2$};
    \node at (2.5,1) {$R_1$};
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \caption{}
    \label{subfig:213_gen_b}
    \end{subfigure}
\caption{The structure of an extendably $213$-avoiding ASR.}
\label{fig:213_gen}
\end{figure}

In both cases, $R_1$ is a row-complete, extendably $213$-avoiding ASR and $R_2$ is a balanced extendably $213$-avoiding ASR (after possibly replacing the $-1$ in the bottom left corner by a $0$). This implies that $i$ has to be at least $r-d+2$.

If row~$i$ does not contain a $-1$ (\cref{subfig:213_gen_a}), then $R_1$ is of size $(r-i) \times (k-d+r-i)$ 
and $R_2$ is of size $(i-1) \times (d-r+i-1)$, which gives
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=r-d+2}^{r} S^{213}_{{r-i},{k-d+r-i},{r-i}} \cdot S^{213}_{{i-1},{d-r+i-1},{d-r+i-1}}
\end{equation*}
possibilities.

On the other hand, if row~$i$ contains a $-1$ (\cref{subfig:213_gen_b}), $R_1$ is again of size $(r-i) \times (k-d+r-i)$. Furthermore, $R_2$ is of size $i \times (d-r+i)$. Since we have to replace the $-1$ by a $0$ in order to obtain a proper extendably $213$-avoiding ASR, we have to count all ASRs of size $i \times (d-r+i)$ that do not have a $1$ in the bottom left corner and that do not have an empty bottom row. In total, we get
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=r-d+2}^{r} S^{213}_{{r-i},{k-d+r-i},{r-i}} \left( S^{213}_{{i},{d-r+i},{d-r+i}} - S^{213}_{{i-1},{d-r+i-1},{d-r+i-1}} - \binom{i-1}{d-r+i} \right)
\end{equation*}
possibilities in that case.

By summing all possibilities, we again obtain the desired identity.

Assume that row~$i$ is the first empty row of an extendably $231$-avoiding ASR of size $r \times k$. Since there will be a $1$ somewhere in row~$i$ of any extension, the ASR looks as in \cref{fig:231_d=k}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]

\draw[fill=lightgray] (0,0) rectangle (5,7);
\draw[fill=white]  (3,0) rectangle (5,3);
\draw[fill=white]  (0,4) rectangle (3,7);

\node at (-1,3.5) {$i$};
\node at (1.5,5.5) {$M$};
\node at (4,1.5) {$R$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The structure of an extendably $231$-avoiding ASR with empty rows.}
\label{fig:231_d=k}
\end{figure}

The top left corner $M$ is a $231$-avoiding ASM of size $(i-1) \times (i-1)$ and $R$ is an extendably $231$-avoiding ASR of size $(r-i) \times (k-i+1)$ with $d-i+1$ nonempty rows. The assertion then follows immediately.

\label{thm:r=d}
    For $2 \le r \le k$, we have
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:thm_r=d}
    S^{312}_{{r},{k},{r}}=\sum_{j=2}^{r} a_{r-1,j}\binom{k-r+j}{j}.
    \end{equation}

This identity is proven by induction on $r$. For the base case, \cref{lem:r=d} implies $S^{312}_{{1},{k},{1}}=k$ and consequently $S^{312}_{{2},{k},{2}}=(k-1)k$, which coincides with
    \[
    \sum_{j=2}^2 a_{1,j}\binom{k-2+j}{j}= a_{1,2}\binom{k}{2} = (k-1)k.
    \]
    For the induction step, we assume $r \ge 3$ and deduce from \cref{lem:r=d} that
    \begin{multline*}
        S^{312}_{{r},{k},{r}} - S^{312}_{{r},{k-1},{r}} = S^{312}_{{r-1},{k-1},{r-1}} + \sum_{i=2}^{r} \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2} \cdot S^{312}_{{r+1-i},{k+1-i},{r+1-i}}\\
        = \sum_{j=2}^{r-1} a_{r-2,j} \binom{k-r+j}{j} + \sum_{i=2}^{r-1} \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2} \sum_{j=2}^{r+1-i} a_{r-i,j}\binom{k-r+j}{j} + \mathrm{Sch}_{r-2} (k-r+1)\\
        = \sum_{j=2}^{r-1} \binom{k-r+j}{j} \left( a_{r-2,j} + \sum_{i=2}^{r+1-j} \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2} a_{r-i,j} \right) + \mathrm{Sch}_{r-2} (k-r+1),
    \end{multline*}
    where we keep in mind that \eqref{eq:thm_r=d} only holds true for $r \ge 2$. By using identity~\eqref{Sch_refinement} and
    \begin{equation*}
        \sum_{i=2}^{r+1-j} \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2} a_{r-i,j} = \sum_{i=2}^{r} \mathrm{Sch}_{i-2} a_{r-i,j}
    \end{equation*}
    because $a_{r-i,j} = 0$ if $j > r-i+1$, we then obtain
    \begin{equation*}
        \sum_{j=2}^{r-1} \binom{k-r+j}{j} a_{r-1,j+1} + \mathrm{Sch}_{r-2} (k-r+1).
    \end{equation*}

    On the other hand, \eqref{eq:thm_r=d} is equivalent to
    \begin{multline*}
        S^{312}_{{r},{k},{r}} - S^{312}_{{r},{k-1},{r}} = \sum_{j=2}^{r} a_{r-1,j}\binom{k-r+j-1}{j-1}\\
        = \sum_{j=3}^{r} a_{r-1,j}\binom{k-r+j-1}{j-1} +  a_{r-1,2}\binom{k-r+1}{1}\\
        = \sum_{j=2}^{r-1} a_{r-1,j+1}\binom{k-r+j}{j} +  \mathrm{Sch}_{r-2} (k-r+1),
    \end{multline*}
    where we used $a_{i,2}=\mathrm{Sch}_{i-1}$ for $i \ge 2$. This concludes the proof.

\label{thm:Schroeder_ballot}
For $1 \le r \le k$, we have
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{Sch}_{k-1,r-1} = S^{312}_{{r},{k},{r}}-S^{312}_{{r},{k-1},{r}}.
\end{equation*}

\label{cor:312_d=r}
For $1 \le r \le k$, we have
\begin{equation*}
S^{312}_{{r},{k},{r}} = \sum_{i=r-1}^{k-1} \frac{i-r+2}{i+1} \sum_{v=0}^{r-1} \binom{i+1}{r-1-v} \binom{i+v}{v}.
\end{equation*}

For $0\le k<r$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
    S^{312}_{{r},{k},{k}}=\binom{r-1}{k}+\sum_{j=1}^k \binom{r-j-1}{k-j} \sum_{i=j-1}^{k-1} \frac{i-j+2}{i+1} \sum_{v=0}^{j-1} \binom{i+1}{j-1-v} \binom{i+v}{v}.
    \end{equation*}

The bijection~$\Psi$ presented in \cref{sec:ASM} maps $n \times n$ $312$-avoiding ASMs to Schr\"oder paths from $(0,0)$ to $(n-1,n-1)$. Let $\widetilde{\Psi}$ be the corresponding bijection that maps a $312$-avoiding  ASMs $M$ to the path obtained by reflecting $\Psi(M)$ along the diagonal~$y=x$; we call these paths \emph{superdiagonal Schr\"oder paths} in contrast to the \emph{subdiagonal Schr\"oder paths} defined earlier.

